Subject: Read every PIC way in the compiled field fast path

What this does
  Adds the number of caching ways a PIC site owns to the AOT layout and probes
  all of them in the generated field-read fast path, not just the primary.

Why we need it
  A polymorphic site installs its second and later shapes in way 1 and up, at
  `way * site_count + site`, so the primary entry keeps the first shape. The
  compiled fast path looked only at the primary, so on a two-shape site it
  missed on every access to the other shape and took the op call into the
  engine's handler. That is why a polymorphic field read was the slowest class:
  callgrind showed 58% of a compiled `field_poly` run in
  `regvm_s_ROP_get_field` and 10% in `qjs_aot_step`, against 21% in the compiled
  body itself.

  The interpreter's `regvm_get_field_ic` already walks the ways (`e += cnt`
  twice), so this makes the compiled path match it. The stride is the function's
  site count, which the generated code takes from `e2_pic_sites()`.

Measured
  `field_poly` 1.65x -> 0.40x against the interpreter (m1.sh classes, full
  micro.js unit): the class now beats the interpreter instead of losing to it.

--- a/quickjs-aot-rt.h
+++ b/quickjs-aot-rt.h
@@ -54,6 +54,10 @@
        ABI constants it compares against (the entry kind, the no-site marker and
        the lazy-JSON tag). */
     uint32_t sizeof_JSRegPIC;
+    /* The number of caching ways a site owns. A polymorphic site installs its
+       later shapes in way 1 and up, so a reader that looks only at way 0 misses
+       them; the inline read probes every way, as the interpreter does. */
+    uint32_t reg_pic_ways;
     uint32_t off_regpic_shape, off_regpic_slot_off, off_regpic_kind;
     uint32_t reg_pic_own, reg_pic_proto, reg_ic_none, tag_lazy_json;
     /* The binary sub-operations the inline arithmetic path names, from
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -60489,6 +60489,7 @@
     out->off_jsvalue_tag = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSValue, tag);
     out->off_jsvalue_u = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSValue, u);
     out->sizeof_JSRegPIC = (uint32_t)sizeof(JSRegPIC);
+    out->reg_pic_ways = (uint32_t)REGVM_PIC_WAYS;
     out->off_regpic_shape = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSRegPIC, shape);
     out->off_regpic_slot_off = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSRegPIC, slot_off);
     out->off_regpic_kind = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSRegPIC, kind);
